WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:05.192
[JINGLE]

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:12.650
OK, good afternoon, and welcome
to "Catching the R Wave, How

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.280
R and RStudio are
Revolutionizing Statistics

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:20.750
Education at Community
Colleges and Beyond."

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:22.190
So a little bit about me--

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:31.160
I've been teaching
for 25 years plus.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.010
Various hats that I've worn
throughout those years--

00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.110
I've taught K through 12.

00:00:36.110 --> 00:00:38.990
I've taught at
four-year college,

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:41.630
and I've taught at
two-year college.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.730
I've taught computer science.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:48.290
I've taught mathematics
at all of the introductory

00:00:48.290 --> 00:00:50.120
undergraduate levels.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:54.890
So it's a lot of teaching.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.010
I spent five years at
Great Bay Community College

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.960
in Portsmouth, New Hampshire.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:02.690
And while there, I was the
chair of the Math Department

00:01:02.690 --> 00:01:05.600
and in charge of the
Engineering Program.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.570
And I also created one of the
nation's first certificates

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:14.285
in practical data science
at a community college.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.580
Also, I'm married
to Harbormaster,

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:21.200
if you know him, on Twitter.

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:29.810
So I have created a
GitHub that contains

00:01:29.810 --> 00:01:32.930
all of the materials that
are relevant to this talk.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:34.490
It's also a work in progress.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.070
I'm actually trying
to put together

00:01:37.070 --> 00:01:42.380
some materials that can turn
into one, cohesive thing

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.470
that a teacher can then
just use as a handbook

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:48.950
for any stats class
and any textbook

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.420
that they might choose to use.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:55.670
So just a quick survey--

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:59.270
raise your hand if you've either
attended a community college

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:02.270
or have taught at a
community college.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:05.030
Wow, it's more than
I was expecting.

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:05.825
That's really cool.

00:02:08.570 --> 00:02:09.770
It was about half the room.

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:14.750
So let's do just a real,
quick overview of just

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:19.280
of a history of why I became
passionate about community

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:21.590
college education.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.360
Why is this such a big deal?

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:32.780
There are over 1,000 community
colleges in the United States.

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:43.460
That's a map of them made
in R. Only 20 of them--

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:44.210
maybe a few more.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.850
We keep adding a
couple every year,

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:51.200
but it's under 20 that actually
have a program right now,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.240
whether it's or certificate,
or an associate's

00:02:53.240 --> 00:02:56.750
degree, or something, that's
either called data science

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.980
or data analytics.

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.980
Compared to the over 1,000,
we have a little bit of a ways

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:02.480
to go.

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:10.870
As a comparison, we did
not make this graph in R.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:15.430
This was copied off
of a report that

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:16.780
was put out by the government.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:21.130
But compared two-year
to four-year colleges,

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.850
we have about 36%
of all students

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:29.140
who were undergraduates
in the year 2016.

00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:33.700
36% were in two-year colleges--
which is more than 1/3.

00:03:33.700 --> 00:03:37.000
And it's kind of surprising
to hear that number.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.940
Most people think that
it's not quite that much,

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:42.381
because we think about all
the undergraduate colleges

00:03:42.381 --> 00:03:42.880
out there.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:44.530
And you're like,
well, it can't be.

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:45.220
But it is.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.760
And as far as the
numbers of students

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:53.740
who receive bachelor's degrees--

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:00.580
I think this was 2015/16.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.680
Of all the bachelor's
degrees awarded in that year,

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.770
49% of those students
had started out

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:11.220
at a community college--

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:15.580
49%, almost 1/2.

00:04:15.580 --> 00:04:18.089
State by state, it does differ.

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:25.570
The highest percentage is Texas,
where we are right now, at 75%.

00:04:25.570 --> 00:04:30.730
So they're really pushing
community college education.

00:04:30.730 --> 00:04:34.630
The lowest is
Rhode Island at 24.

00:04:34.630 --> 00:04:35.870
You can barely see it there.

00:04:35.870 --> 00:04:38.440
But it's there.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:43.180
So why did I become
passionate about this?

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.210
Because what
happens is a student

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:47.740
comes out of high school--

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:54.700
and even adults who might want
to go back for a credential--

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.500
but especially high
school students.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:04:59.230
Let's focus on
them for a second.

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:01.090
They start out at a
community college.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:05.740
And they get a list of programs
that they could sign up for.

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:09.430
And very few of them--
or not many of them--

00:05:09.430 --> 00:05:13.030
are really comfortable
with math or science.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.550
You have few engineering
people who just are

00:05:15.550 --> 00:05:17.980
determined to do engineering.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:21.250
That's kind of a unique crowd.

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:25.430
But most students
pick a program,

00:05:25.430 --> 00:05:27.190
and then they stick with that.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.980
They're not going to
diverge from that--

00:05:29.980 --> 00:05:32.290
hence, the road.

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:35.530
And what I would
like to see is I

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:37.720
would like to see
more students exposed

00:05:37.720 --> 00:05:40.960
to data science in
the community college

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.810
setting so that then,
when they transfer

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:48.460
to a four-year college,
they're sort of on a track

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:51.520
to continue that kind
of work, and maybe

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:56.230
do research-driven work, and
maybe choose a STEM major.

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:06.640
So I just wanted to show you the
program sheet for the Practical

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:11.200
Data Science as it exists
right now at Great Bay.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:13.990
Great Bay Community
College is located

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:15.980
in Portsmouth, New Hampshire.

00:06:15.980 --> 00:06:18.250
It's about an hour
north of Boston.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:21.550
So I was able to use the
fact that it's close enough

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:25.840
to Boston as a sort
of justification

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:27.715
for creating the program.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:33.910
All of those courses are
liberal arts courses.

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:36.220
They can transfer to
any four-year college

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:37.570
as a liberal arts course.

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:43.870
I deliberately got them
categorized as that.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:51.850
So along with this
work that I was

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:57.970
doing to create the certificate,
I was also thinking,

00:06:57.970 --> 00:07:00.190
how can we make it
easier for students

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:06.280
to make the transition from
Excel, or from their Texas

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:07.870
Instruments--

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.240
whatever technology
they're used to--

00:07:10.240 --> 00:07:16.510
how do we get them comfortable
with R, especially in that--

00:07:16.510 --> 00:07:20.350
the first course that I teach
in the data science curriculum

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.660
is called Elements
of Data Science.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:27.320
And typically, it takes three
weeks out of a 15-week semester

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:30.190
just to get students
up and running in R,

00:07:30.190 --> 00:07:32.080
believe it or not.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:34.030
So I'm seeing a lot of nods.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:40.030
Well, so what if we
started introducing R

00:07:40.030 --> 00:07:43.060
in some of our other
coursework, like even

00:07:43.060 --> 00:07:46.330
in a biology class, or
in a chemistry class,

00:07:46.330 --> 00:07:47.750
or a physics class?

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:51.130
And then, in my case, since
I was in charge of the Math

00:07:51.130 --> 00:07:53.590
Department, I could point
to the statistics class

00:07:53.590 --> 00:07:56.950
and tell all my
statistics instructors,

00:07:56.950 --> 00:08:00.160
this is what we're
going to do, and this

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:03.010
is how we're going to do it.

00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:04.560
And I'm with you.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:05.640
I'm supporting you.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:06.720
I'm going to teach you.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.530
We're going to work
on this together.

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:11.640
We're going to
stumble a little bit.

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:14.070
The first couple of
times we tried it,

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:17.640
it was terrible--
and especially here.

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:21.120
Because in 2014, that's
when we started doing it.

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.880
And the feedback we were
getting from students

00:08:23.880 --> 00:08:28.470
was, oh, man, you're
asking me to learn

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:31.770
programming and statistics
at the same time,

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.690
and I can't do it.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:36.730
It's too hard.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:44.520
But fast forward to 2018,
2019 and now I'm like,

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:47.410
wow, how can we get
R into the classroom?

00:08:47.410 --> 00:08:49.080
How can we get it
into more classrooms?

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:50.050
It's really cool.

00:08:50.050 --> 00:08:56.190
It's really working--
so, very enthusiastic.

00:08:56.190 --> 00:08:59.610
And just a shout out
for RStudio Cloud here--

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.580
RStudio Cloud has
been invaluable

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:05.520
for the Elements of
Data Science class.

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:08.310
I don't use it in
the Statistics class.

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:11.580
And after the talk, if you
would like to know why,

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.580
I can explain.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:18.210
But RStudio Cloud is used in
our Elements of Data Science.

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:25.470
And most of the course
outcomes involve

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:30.450
wrangling data, finding
the data sets that

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.850
will answer a question
that you want to answer,

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:38.010
and then coming up with that
tidy data set at the end.

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:42.120
And it takes a whole
semester to do that.

00:09:42.120 --> 00:09:43.200
We'll just be honest.

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:54.280
But before we talk about
how to incorporate R

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:56.020
into the statistics
classroom, let's

00:09:56.020 --> 00:09:59.410
talk a little bit about why.

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:00.970
So here's an example.

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:05.350
Actually, this was a
slide from a lecture

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:09.580
that Pearson Publishing
produces for instructors

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:11.710
of just intro stats.

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:17.260
So this is just a page out
of a lecture for intro stats.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:21.580
And yes, you're going
to learn something.

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:25.300
If you answer this
question correctly,

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.040
you've learned something.

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.804
But have you learned what
we want you to learn?

00:10:30.804 --> 00:10:31.720
What have you learned?

00:10:31.720 --> 00:10:33.880
You've learned how
to use a formula,

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:38.380
how to convert from
an x to a z value.

00:10:38.380 --> 00:10:40.660
So I know how to use a formula.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.940
And you've learned how
to use the z-table.

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:47.440
That's it.

00:10:47.440 --> 00:10:50.230
There's no kinds
of questioning here

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.660
that involves any
kind of deep learning

00:10:52.660 --> 00:10:55.870
or any kind of analysis.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:58.000
Why are we getting
what we're getting--

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.910
just nothing like that.

00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:09.830
So the Math Department at
Great Bay Community College--

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.180
namely, myself.

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:15.890
And just so you know, I'm
a collaborative leader.

00:11:15.890 --> 00:11:19.700
I was not just somebody who
said, we're going to do this.

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:21.140
We all met together.

00:11:21.140 --> 00:11:23.630
I met with all of my adjuncts.

00:11:23.630 --> 00:11:28.160
And I said, OK, here's
what we're faced with.

00:11:28.160 --> 00:11:30.800
This is this is what
I'm thinking about.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:31.760
What do you think?

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.040
And just getting
everybody on board with it

00:11:34.040 --> 00:11:37.730
is a big, big, big deal.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:41.110
Getting your stakeholders
and the people

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:44.690
that you're going to be
asking to do all of the work--

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:47.060
it's important to
get them on board

00:11:47.060 --> 00:11:49.520
and to get them
enthusiastic about it,

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:54.020
or you're not going
to go very far.

00:11:54.020 --> 00:11:58.280
But anyway, we decided
to make technology period

00:11:58.280 --> 00:12:02.480
a big part of the curriculum
throughout the Math Department.

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:05.750
So one of the one of my
favorite courses to teach

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.610
is Finite Math.

00:12:07.610 --> 00:12:11.720
You do input/output analysis.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:13.730
There's these things
that you can actually

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.670
do in R in a finite math class.

00:12:16.670 --> 00:12:19.640
It doesn't even have
to be statistics.

00:12:19.640 --> 00:12:24.785
And we were doing matrices
in R. It makes sense.

00:12:30.380 --> 00:12:32.915
Whenever a student
comes up to me and says,

00:12:32.915 --> 00:12:38.090
ugh, it's too hard,
I just point to this.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.475
I'm like, and that's easy?

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:46.880
Tell me what about this is easy.

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:48.870
And what about it is--

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.080
OK, I've got this
tiny little thing.

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:56.012
If I happened to
be sight impaired,

00:12:56.012 --> 00:12:57.470
there's no way that
I'm going to be

00:12:57.470 --> 00:13:01.700
able to get to read anything
off of that tiny little screen

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:03.020
on that Casio.

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:06.586
If you've never seen one of
those Casio calculators--

00:13:06.586 --> 00:13:08.210
I should have put a
picture up for you.

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:16.810
Whenever I show instructors--

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:20.050
when I am in a room with a
bunch of community college

00:13:20.050 --> 00:13:23.530
instructors and I
show them this--

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:25.340
and I'm going to
be honest with you.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:29.169
I usually go to
that R-D-I-O dot--

00:13:29.169 --> 00:13:29.710
I don't know.

00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:31.120
It's a snippets thing.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:34.660
So I usually just go there.

00:13:34.660 --> 00:13:37.390
And I Copy and Paste this in.

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.290
Boom, here comes the output.

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.790
And I'm like, you can do this.

00:13:42.790 --> 00:13:43.840
You can do this.

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:45.850
And usually, the
instructors are--

00:13:45.850 --> 00:13:50.930
wow, can I do that on my phone?

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:53.170
And I say, well,
yeah, because you

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:54.400
have a browser on your phone.

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:56.890
If it's a smartphone,
you have a browser.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:14:02.350
So you bring up the
window that actually has

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:05.380
that executable window in it.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:08.560
Just Copy and Paste--
and yeah, of course.

00:14:08.560 --> 00:14:10.200
So, they're amazed.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:13.195
And they begin to
buy into this idea.

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:21.010
And I'm also going
to admit, this

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:24.250
is an actual image of
a spreadsheet template

00:14:24.250 --> 00:14:28.690
that I used in my
Intro Stats class.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:35.155
Apologies to anybody
who had me at that time.

00:14:41.680 --> 00:14:44.470
It was designed so that you
could just put in the values

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:48.370
that you needed to, and out
would come all of the value.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:50.800
So you didn't have
to use a table.

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:57.250
And I'm sorry, but after
that talk this morning--

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:00.010
you heard the keynote, right?

00:15:00.010 --> 00:15:02.680
This is programming.

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:04.180
I just want to put
that out there.

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:05.320
This is programming.

00:15:09.550 --> 00:15:14.770
The same output in R with
only a few lines of code

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:17.872
basically is just-- and
you'll see this in my GitHub.

00:15:17.872 --> 00:15:19.330
The code for this
is in the GitHub.

00:15:22.270 --> 00:15:24.970
It's just amazing when
I show the contrast

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:34.480
with educators about how
much more use you can

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:35.740
get with this kind of output.

00:15:40.770 --> 00:15:42.720
So here I was--

00:15:42.720 --> 00:15:46.110
at that point, when
we had the cloud

00:15:46.110 --> 00:15:49.260
and I was using just these
little snippets of code,

00:15:49.260 --> 00:15:50.160
I was all excited.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.860
And now all of the
sudden, Shiny is here.

00:15:52.860 --> 00:15:56.580
And now I have to admit, I
haven't played with Shiny yet.

00:15:56.580 --> 00:16:01.300
But I'm getting the point
across from many people

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:02.430
that I need to.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:05.720
So everyone's telling me
yeah, you really need to.

00:16:05.720 --> 00:16:13.980
But it's actually going to be
helping in this whole effort

00:16:13.980 --> 00:16:16.650
to try to get community
college instructors

00:16:16.650 --> 00:16:18.180
on the board with us.

00:16:18.180 --> 00:16:22.590
Because Cal Poly, Penn State--

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:24.030
and I'm sure there's more--

00:16:24.030 --> 00:16:28.470
they have published Shiny
apps that are specifically

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:32.250
for the intro stats class.

00:16:32.250 --> 00:16:35.800
And hey, I'm going
to check them out.

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:46.530
And then, in addition to that,
StatPREP is an effort that

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:50.280
began as a collaboration between
the Mathematical Association

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:57.810
of America and then this
organization called AMATYC,

00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:00.750
which is American Mathematical
Association of Two-Year

00:17:00.750 --> 00:17:01.800
Colleges--

00:17:01.800 --> 00:17:03.960
AMATYC.

00:17:03.960 --> 00:17:08.400
And then also NSF is supporting
the funding for this.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:12.540
And ASA, as well, is involved.

00:17:12.540 --> 00:17:15.030
And what are they doing?

00:17:15.030 --> 00:17:18.060
They have these
traveling workshops

00:17:18.060 --> 00:17:22.440
that are at various
locations around the country.

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:42.870
Anybody can apply if you are
an undergraduate educator.

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:44.520
You don't have to be full time.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:46.170
You can be part time.

00:17:46.170 --> 00:17:48.030
You can just apply.

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:50.730
And look to see if
there's a workshop that's

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:52.480
somewhat close to you.

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:57.584
But you can actually get a
whole group of people to go.

00:17:57.584 --> 00:17:58.500
That's what they want.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:07.080
They want colleges to send
a whole team of educators.

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:11.400
And I put my own text over
there, so you could read it.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:13.200
The next webinar is coming up.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:16.260
It's this Friday, January 25.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:21.720
And it's about
introducing students

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:23.400
to multi-variable
data-- students who've

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:25.500
never seen it before.

00:18:25.500 --> 00:18:31.140
So it's a really good
effort that Katie is doing.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:33.960
And if you go under the
About and the People--

00:18:33.960 --> 00:18:37.080
it's all on my GitHub,
as far as who's involved

00:18:37.080 --> 00:18:40.320
and how you can get
involved to help, as well.

00:18:44.770 --> 00:18:49.810
One of the Shiny apps that
the StatPREP team uses is--

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:54.550
this is just an example for you
where they use these Shiny apps

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:55.480
for their training.

00:18:55.480 --> 00:19:01.270
So they're actually showing
instructors what a function is,

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:04.000
what parameters are.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:06.670
What are these things
inside of the parentheses?

00:19:06.670 --> 00:19:09.820
And seriously, if
you've ever used Excel,

00:19:09.820 --> 00:19:14.290
there's a natural connection
between what goes on

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:18.400
with functions in Excel and what
goes on with functions in R.

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:21.240
It's not that difficult
to make that transition.

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:28.900
Also, the American
Statistical Association

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:31.780
has provided materials, as well.

00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:33.610
There's data.

00:19:33.610 --> 00:19:37.530
If you're looking
for data, go there.

00:19:37.530 --> 00:19:38.540
Here's what we need.

00:19:38.540 --> 00:19:42.030
We need your help.

00:19:42.030 --> 00:19:44.040
Community colleges
need your help.

00:19:44.040 --> 00:19:48.060
If you've ever been in a
community college, you know.

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:49.170
We can make a difference.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:54.240
And we can make data literacy
a reality in our culture.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.640
We want to make smarter,
better educators.

00:19:56.640 --> 00:19:58.950
Go out there to your
local community college.

00:19:58.950 --> 00:20:02.100
Make a relationship with
the instructors there.

00:20:02.100 --> 00:20:03.670
Just start a relationship.

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.040
Start a conversation.

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:07.866
Find out what their needs are--

00:20:07.866 --> 00:20:10.050
a whole lot of
educating going on.

00:20:10.050 --> 00:20:15.000
We want to make all
these dots programs

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:20.850
with something relating to data
science or some kind of intro.

00:20:20.850 --> 00:20:24.630
And if you're a
developer, make apps that

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:28.230
are available for any device--

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:31.080
any device.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:39.540
Bottom line-- is this the R
experience we want or this?

